  "worked_solution": "In order for ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ to be equal to $8,$ the left and right-sided limits must both exist and be equal to $8.$\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x\\le 2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=a{x}^{2}+2x-3.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}(a{x}^{2}+2x-3) \\\\  & =a({2}^{2})+2(2)-3 \\\\  & =4a+1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left-sided limit must be equal to $9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & =9 \\\\ 4a+1 & =9 \\\\ 4a & =8 \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=4x+b.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(4x+b) \\\\  & =4(2)+b \\\\  & =8+b\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the right-sided limit must be equal to $9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & =9 \\\\ 8+b & =9 \\\\ b & =9-8 \\\\ b & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
